WEBVTT

00:00:00.144 --> 00:00:03.342
One correction is important before we start.

00:00:03.342 --> 00:00:08.737
PostgreSQL does not use DATEADD and DATEDIFF as native functions.

00:00:08.737 --> 00:00:14.282
Instead, PostgreSQL commonly uses date or timestamp subtraction, INTERVAL values, EXTRACT, DATE_TRUNC, AGE,

00:00:14.282 --> 00:00:17.677
TO_DATE, TO_TIMESTAMP, and TO_CHAR.

00:00:17.677 --> 00:00:23.547
The underlying analytical ideas are portable, but the SQL syntax depends on the database system.

00:00:23.547 --> 00:00:28.732
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.732 --> 00:00:34.937
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.937 --> 00:00:38.852
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:38.852 --> 00:00:42.787
In a real setting such as a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers,

00:00:42.787 --> 00:00:50.637
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:50.637 --> 00:00:53.607
before discussing syntax.

00:00:53.607 --> 00:00:59.440
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
